    <div class="row g-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Friendly Introduction</h2>
                <p>In this lesson, we will understand three architecture styles that every ASP.NET Core developer should know: monolithic, layered, and distributed systems. People often hear these terms in interviews, but in real projects they are practical choices, not buzzwords.</p>
                <p class="mb-0">By the end of this lesson, you should be able to explain the differences clearly, identify when each approach fits, and discuss migration decisions with confidence.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">This lesson covers</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>The single unit (Monolith), vertical stack (Layered), and networked approach (Distributed).</li>
                    <li>Scalability differences and blast radius impact.</li>
                    <li>Synchronous HTTP vs asynchronous messaging communication patterns.</li>
                    <li>E-commerce case study and a pizza shop analogy for beginners.</li>
                    <li>Transition guidance, common mistakes, MCQs, and interview Q&amp;A.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Step-by-Step Explanation</h2>
                <p>To make this easier, let us take a simple example: an e-commerce application with catalog, cart, order, payment, and delivery notifications.</p>

                <div class="row g-3 mt-1">
                    <div class="col-lg-4">
                        <div class="arch-card">
                            <h3 class="h5">Step 1: Monolithic (Single Unit)</h3>
                            <p>All code lives in one project or tightly coupled solution and gets deployed as one application unit.</p>
                            <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                                <li>One deployment pipeline.</li>
                                <li>Usually one shared database.</li>
                                <li>Simple to build early on.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-lg-4">
                        <div class="arch-card">
                            <h3 class="h5">Step 2: Layered (Vertical Stack / N-Tier)</h3>
                            <p>The app is still a single deployable, but responsibilities are separated into layers.</p>
                            <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                                <li>UI (Controllers/Views).</li>
                                <li>Service or business logic layer.</li>
                                <li>Data access/repository layer.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-lg-4">
                        <div class="arch-card">
                            <h3 class="h5">Step 3: Distributed (Networked Services)</h3>
                            <p>The system is split into independent services that communicate over the network.</p>
                            <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                                <li>Separate deployments per service.</li>
                                <li>Independent scaling per service.</li>
                                <li>API or messaging based integration.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Architecture Comparison Diagram</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">Monolithic:
[UI + Business + Data + Modules] --> One Deployable App --> One DB

Layered (still one app):
[UI Layer]
    ↓
[Service / Business Layer]
    ↓
[Data Access Layer]
    ↓
[Database]

Distributed:
Client -> API Gateway
            ├-> Catalog Service -> Catalog DB
            ├-> Order Service   -> Order DB
            ├-> Payment Service -> Payment DB
            └-> Notification Service (events)</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Pizza Shop Analogy (Fresher-Friendly)</h2>
                <p>Now let us compare these approaches with a pizza shop analogy, then map it back to software architecture.</p>

                <h3 class="h5">1) Monolith = One-Man Pizza Shop</h3>
                <p>One person takes order, prepares dough, adds toppings, bakes, and delivers. Everything is in one place and easy to coordinate, but if that person is overloaded or unavailable, the whole shop suffers.</p>
                <p><strong>Software mapping:</strong> all responsibilities in one app and one deployment. If one critical issue crashes the app, everything is affected.</p>

                <h3 class="h5">2) Layered = Organized Kitchen in One Building</h3>
                <p>Front Desk handles orders (UI), Kitchen handles preparation (Business Logic), Pantry stores ingredients (Data). Work is organized by responsibility, but all sections still depend on the same building.</p>
                <p><strong>Software mapping:</strong> cleaner structure in one app. Maintainability improves, but if the whole app/server goes down, every layer stops.</p>

                <h3 class="h5">3) Distributed = Pizza Shop Chain / Separate Units</h3>
                <p>One unit prepares dough, another handles toppings, another manages delivery. They coordinate through phone/radio (APIs/events). If delivery is busy, you add more delivery riders without scaling dough preparation.</p>
                <p class="mb-0"><strong>Software mapping:</strong> independent services with network communication. One unit can scale or fail without fully stopping others.</p>

<pre class="diagram-box mt-3">Pizza Analogy Diagram

Monolith:      [One Person: Order + Cook + Pack + Deliver]
Layered:       [Front Desk] -> [Kitchen] -> [Pantry]   (same building)
Distributed:   [Dough Unit] <-> [Toppings Unit] <-> [Delivery Unit]
               (phone/radio communication = API/events)</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Scalability and Blast Radius Visual</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">Traffic Spike Scenario (Festival Sale)

Monolith/Layered:
Need to scale FULL app even if only Orders are hot.
Crash in Payment module can impact complete app runtime.
Blast radius: HIGH

Distributed:
Scale only Order Service + Payment Service.
If Notification Service fails, core checkout can continue with retry queue.
Blast radius: LIMITED / ISOLATED</pre>
                <p class="mt-3 mb-0">This is why distributed systems are chosen for selective scalability and fault isolation, not because they are automatically "modern".</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Communication Patterns: Synchronous vs Asynchronous</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-3">
                    <li><strong>Synchronous HTTP:</strong> one service calls another and waits for immediate response. Good for direct read operations like getting product details.</li>
                    <li><strong>Asynchronous Messaging:</strong> service publishes an event and continues. Good for decoupled tasks like sending email after order placement.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="info-box mb-0">Practical tip: keep customer-facing request flow short with synchronous APIs, and move non-blocking workflows (notifications, analytics, audit logs) to asynchronous messaging.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Real-World Example (E-commerce Transition)</h2>
                <p><strong>Phase 1:</strong> One monolithic ASP.NET Core app handles catalog, orders, payments, and admin.</p>
                <p><strong>Phase 2:</strong> Team refactors to layered architecture (UI, service, data) to reduce code chaos and improve testability.</p>
                <p><strong>Phase 3:</strong> Order and Payment are extracted as services due to high traffic and release bottlenecks. Notifications become event-driven.</p>
                <div class="warning-box mb-0">Transition guidance: start from a healthy layered monolith. Extract one high-impact capability first. Avoid big-bang migration.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Benefits / Strengths</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Monolith:</strong> fastest to start, lower operational overhead.</li>
                    <li><strong>Layered:</strong> clear separation of concerns, easier long-term maintenance.</li>
                    <li><strong>Distributed:</strong> independent scaling, better fault isolation, team autonomy.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Challenges / Limitations</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Monolith:</strong> release risk grows with codebase size.</li>
                    <li><strong>Layered:</strong> still one deployment unit and shared runtime risk.</li>
                    <li><strong>Distributed:</strong> network failures, observability complexity, and data consistency concerns.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
