        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Best Practices, Tips, and Common Mistakes</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-lg-6">
                        <div class="good-box h-100">
                            <h3 class="h6 fw-bold">Best Practices</h3>
                            <ul class="mb-0 list-tight">
                                <li>Start simple; choose architecture based on current business needs.</li>
                                <li>Create clear module boundaries before extracting services.</li>
                                <li>Add resilience patterns: timeout, retry, circuit breaker, fallback.</li>
                                <li>Invest in logs, metrics, traces, and CI/CD early.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-lg-6">
                        <div class="bad-box h-100">
                            <h3 class="h6 fw-bold">Common Mistakes</h3>
                            <ul class="mb-0 list-tight">
                                <li>Jumping to distributed architecture too early.</li>
                                <li>Splitting services by technical layers instead of business domains.</li>
                                <li>Using only synchronous calls and creating service chains.</li>
                                <li>Ignoring data ownership and transaction boundaries.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">Now you can clearly explain that monolithic and layered systems are both single deployment styles, while distributed architecture breaks the system into independent networked services. Monolith is often enough in the beginning, layered architecture helps control code quality as the app grows, and distributed architecture becomes useful when independent scaling, fault isolation, and team autonomy become real needs.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">MCQ Questions with Answers</h2>

                <div class="qa-item">
                    <p><strong>1) Which statement best describes a monolithic architecture?</strong><br />
                        A. Multiple small services deployed independently<br />
                        B. One application containing all modules, deployed as one unit<br />
                        C. A system where each layer is deployed separately<br />
                        D. A system that only uses asynchronous messaging</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> B<br /><strong>Explanation:</strong> In monoliths, the full application is packaged and released together.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="qa-item">
                    <p><strong>2) In a layered architecture, what usually changes first?</strong><br />
                        A. Deployment style<br />
                        B. Team structure only<br />
                        C. Internal code organization by responsibility<br />
                        D. Database vendor</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> C<br /><strong>Explanation:</strong> Layered architecture improves separation of concerns while still keeping a single deployable app.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="qa-item">
                    <p><strong>3) Why do distributed systems scale better for specific hotspots?</strong><br />
                        A. Because all services share one process<br />
                        B. Because only required services can be scaled independently<br />
                        C. Because they avoid databases
                        <br />D. Because they never fail</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> B<br /><strong>Explanation:</strong> You can scale only high-traffic services like Order or Payment instead of scaling the entire application.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="qa-item">
                    <p><strong>4) What does blast radius mean in architecture discussions?</strong><br />
                        A. Number of users who like the UI<br />
                        B. Memory usage of the application<br />
                        C. How widely a failure spreads across the system<br />
                        D. The speed of code compilation</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> C<br /><strong>Explanation:</strong> Blast radius describes failure impact scope—high in monoliths, lower in well-designed distributed systems.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="qa-item">
                    <p><strong>5) Which communication style is best for non-blocking actions like sending order confirmation emails?</strong><br />
                        A. Synchronous HTTP only<br />
                        B. In-process direct method call from UI<br />
                        C. Asynchronous messaging/events<br />
                        D. Manual database polling from client</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> C<br /><strong>Explanation:</strong> Asynchronous messaging decouples services and improves resilience for background workflows.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="qa-item">
                    <p><strong>6) What is a safe migration sequence for many teams?</strong><br />
                        A. Monolith → 40 microservices in one month<br />
                        B. Layered monolith → extract one high-value service → continue incrementally<br />
                        C. Distributed first, then monolith<br />
                        D. Skip architecture planning</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> B<br /><strong>Explanation:</strong> Incremental extraction lowers risk and helps teams learn operational patterns gradually.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Interview Questions and Answers</h2>
                <div class="accordion" id="interviewQaAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="iq1"><button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ia1" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="ia1">1) Monolith and layered are both single deployments. Then why use layered at all?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ia1" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="iq1" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Layered architecture improves readability, testability, and maintainability by separating concerns. It is often the most practical step before any service extraction.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="iq2"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ia2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ia2">2) When is a monolith enough?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ia2" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="iq2" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">A monolith is enough for early-stage products, small teams, and moderate traffic where fast delivery and low operational complexity matter more than service-level scaling.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="iq3"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ia3" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ia3">3) How would you explain blast radius to a non-technical stakeholder?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ia3" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="iq3" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Blast radius means how much of the business stops when one part fails. In monoliths, one severe issue can affect everything. In distributed systems, a failure can be isolated to one capability.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="iq4"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ia4" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ia4">4) Why mix synchronous HTTP and asynchronous messaging in distributed systems?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ia4" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="iq4" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Synchronous HTTP is good for immediate responses. Asynchronous messaging is better for eventual workflows and loose coupling. Using both provides balance between responsiveness and resilience.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="iq5"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ia5" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ia5">5) What are warning signs that your team should consider distributed architecture?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ia5" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="iq5" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Frequent release conflicts, inability to scale only hot modules, long regression cycles, and multiple teams blocking each other are strong signs.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="iq6"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ia6" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ia6">6) What is one practical interview answer for migration strategy?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ia6" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="iq6" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Start with a modular layered monolith, identify one domain with clear ROI (like Payment), extract it first, and support it with observability, API contracts, and rollback-ready deployment pipelines.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
